WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:04.810
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.810 --> 00:00:08.240
Today we are going to burn some virtual trees
down.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.030
This is a fantastic computer graphics paper
from four years ago.

00:00:12.030 --> 00:00:18.270
I ask you to hold on to your papers immediately,
and do not get surprised if it spontaneously

00:00:18.270 --> 00:00:19.760
lights on fire.

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:26.240
Yes, this work is about simulating wood combustion,
and it is one of my favorite kinds of papers

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:31.950
that takes an extremely narrow task, and absolutely
nails it.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:36.420
Everything we can possibly ask for from such
a simulation is there.

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:43.399
Each leaf has its own individual mass and
area, they burn individually, transfer heat

00:00:43.399 --> 00:00:50.600
to their surroundings, and finally, branches
bend, and, look can eventually even break

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:54.409
in this process.

00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:59.899
If we look under the hood, we see that these
trees are defined as a system of connected

00:00:59.899 --> 00:01:04.800
particles embedded within a physics simulator.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.590
These particles have their own properties,
for instance, you see the temperature changes

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:15.600
here at different regions of the tree as the
fire gradually consumes it.

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.990
Now if you have been holding on to your papers,
now, squeeze that paper and look.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:24.590
What do you think - is this fire movement
pre-programmed?

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:28.820
It doesn’t seem like it.

00:01:28.820 --> 00:01:34.410
This seems more like some real-time mouse
movement, which is great news indeed, and,

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:39.790
yes, that means that this simulation, and
all the interactions we can do with it runs

00:01:39.790 --> 00:01:43.630
in real time.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:48.110
Here is a list of the many quantities it can
simulate, oh my goodness!

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:53.030
There is so much yummy physics here I don’t
even know where to start.

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:58.300
Let’s pick the water content here and see
how changing it would look.

00:01:58.300 --> 00:02:07.770
This is a tree with a lower water content,
it catches fire rather easily, and now, let’s

00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:09.630
pour some rain on it.

00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:16.269
Then, afterwards, look, it becomes much more
difficult to light on fire and emits huge

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:18.840
plumes of dense, dense smoke.

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:19.840
Beautiful.

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:25.439
And, we can even play with these parameters
in real time.

00:02:25.439 --> 00:02:30.860
We can also have a ton of fun by choosing
non-physical parameters for the breaking coefficient,

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:37.349
which, of course, can lead to the tree suddenly
falling apart in a non-physical way.

00:02:37.349 --> 00:02:41.719
The cool thing here is that we can either
set these parameters to physically plausible

00:02:41.719 --> 00:02:48.609
values and get a really realistic simulation,
or, we can choose to bend reality in directions

00:02:48.609 --> 00:02:51.739
that are in line with our artistic vision.

00:02:51.739 --> 00:02:53.489
How cool is that?

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:56.219
I could play with this all day.

00:02:56.219 --> 00:03:03.890
So, as an experienced Scholar, you ask, okay,
this looks great, but how good are these simulations,

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:04.890
really?

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:11.060
Are they just good enough to fool the untrained
eye, or are they indeed close to reality?

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:13.200
I hope you know what’s coming.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:18.329
Because what is coming is my favorite part
in all simulation research, and that is when

00:03:18.329 --> 00:03:23.480
we let reality be our judge and compare the
simulation to that.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:30.169
This is a piece of real footage of a piece
of burning wood, and this is the simulation.

00:03:30.169 --> 00:03:35.730
Well, we see that the resolution of the fire
simulation was a little limited, it was four

00:03:35.730 --> 00:03:42.150
years ago after all, however, it runs very
similarly to the real life footage.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:43.319
Bravo!

00:03:43.319 --> 00:03:46.819
And all this was done in 2017.

00:03:46.819 --> 00:03:48.520
What a time to be alive!

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:55.109
But we are not even close to be done yet,
this paper teaches us one more important lesson.

00:03:55.109 --> 00:04:01.939
After publishing such an incredible work,
it was accepted to SIGGRAPH ASIA 2017.

00:04:01.939 --> 00:04:07.049
That is one of the most prestigious conferences
in this research field, getting a paper accepted

00:04:07.049 --> 00:04:12.519
here is equivalent to winning the olympic
gold medal of computer graphics research.

00:04:12.519 --> 00:04:18.569
So with that, we would expect that the authors
now revel in eternal glory.

00:04:18.569 --> 00:04:19.569
Right?

00:04:19.569 --> 00:04:22.410
Well, let’s see.

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:23.410
What?

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:24.880
Is this serious?

00:04:24.880 --> 00:04:30.160
The original video was seen by less than a
thousand people online.

00:04:30.160 --> 00:04:31.889
How can that be?

00:04:31.889 --> 00:04:37.139
And the paper was referred to only ten times
by other works in these four years.

00:04:37.139 --> 00:04:43.840
Now, you see, that is not so bad in computer
graphics at all, it is an order, maybe even

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.949
orders of magnitude smaller field than machine
learning, but I think this is an excellent

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:52.319
demonstration of why I started this series.

00:04:52.319 --> 00:04:57.780
And it is because I get so excited by these
incredible human achievements, and I feel

00:04:57.780 --> 00:05:02.620
that they deserve a little more love than
they are given, and of course, these are so

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:05.979
amazing, everybody has to know about them.

00:05:05.979 --> 00:05:10.580
Happy to have you Fellow Scholars watching
this and celebrating these papers with me

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:13.550
for more than 500 episodes now.

00:05:13.550 --> 00:06:09.740
Thank you so much, it is a true honor to have
such an amazing and receptive audience.

00:06:09.740 --> 00:06:19.050
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

